  "query_preview": "..\nHigh pressure is currently centered over northern\nNew Mexico and the Texas Panhandle where it will stay through\nthe middle of the week. Later today deterministic models show \nan inverted trough moving through northern Sonora which will \nenhance mid-level flow leading to rather strong 0-6 km shear \nvalues of 25-35 knots across southeast Arizona. This feature \nwill help focus today's convection along the international \nborder and Sonora. Storm development is also expected in the \nWhite Mountains as well. This is depicted by hi-res guidance \nwith most storm activity likely south and east of Tucson. Tucson\nand central Pima County will likely be on the northern and \nwestern end of storm coverage. Thanks to the wind shear would \nnot be surprised to see some rotating storms and as a result \nsevere wind gusts. SPC has most of southeast Arizona under a \nmarginal risk for severe weather today. Storms will also be \ncapable of heavy rainfall with localized flash flooding possible\nas well. Although fast storm movement may limit the flash flood\nthreat some. \n\nMonday night into Tuesday the inverted trough moves northward \nthrough western Arizona. Mean HREF and REFS 0-6 km shear stays \naround 25-35 kts again on Tuesday with isolated to scattered \nstorms along the AZ/NM border and international border. Again \nwith the shear in place some storms could have briefly rotating \nupdrafts leading to strong to severe outflow winds. Blowing dust\nalong I-10 in Cochise County will also be possible hazard during\nthe afternoon and evening.\n\nWednesday favored areas for isolated storm development will\nagain be to the south and east of Tucson. High temperatures\ntoday into Wednesday will run 2-6 degrees above normal. After\nWednesday the ridge begins to shift westward and becomes\ncentered over central/northern Arizona and also strengthens. \nThis could suppress storm activity Thursday with the current NBM\nforecast only showing chances in the White Mountains. Storm \nchances then increase Friday into the weekend with storm chances\nextending further westward to Tucson and central Pima County by\nthis weekend. \n\nWith the ridge almost overhead extreme heat will become a threat\nstarting Thursday until at least Saturday. Mean NAEFS 500 mb \nheights are around 600 dam over Arizona during this time period,\nwhich is a maximum in the CFSR climatology. High temperatures \nwill be 7-12 degrees above normal with Tucson again flirting \nwith 110 degrees. Right now Friday looks be the best chance to \nhit 110 with NBM probabilities at the Tucson airport around 60 \npercent. This will lead to areas of Major and Extreme HeatRisk,\nwhich will likely necessitate the need for heat headlines.",
